>>__ load "simpLib";
>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `threaded_cong_ss`

``` hol4
simpLib.threaded_cong_ss : thm list -> simpLib.ssfrag
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Creates a fragment of threaded congruence rules.

In a congruence rule (see `simpLib.SSFRAG`), the variables
standing for simplified subterms occur in the conclusion. In a threaded
rule, such a variable may instead be used only in a later antecedent,
which combines earlier results into the final one. For example, `Q` and
`g` below are the results of the first two simplifications, and the third
antecedent uses them to produce the result `h`:

``` hol4
   |- (!x. P x = Q x) ==> (!x. Q x ==> (f x = g x)) ==>
      (!x. (Q x /\ g x) = h x) ==>
      ((?x. P x /\ f x) = (?x. h x))
```

Beta-redexes created by instantiating higher-order variables in the
right-hand side of the conclusion are reduced. `threaded_cong_ss ths` is an
unnamed fragment containing the rules `ths`; congruence rules given to
`simpLib.SSFRAG` or `simpLib.Cong` are not threaded.

### Failure

Never fails. Using a malformed rule can make adding the fragment, or
simplifying with the result, fail.

### Example

``` hol4
>>_ val threaded = prove(
      “(!x:'a. P x = Q x) ==> (!x:'a. Q x ==> (f x = g x)) ==>
       (!x:'a. (Q x /\ g x) = h x) ==>
       ((?x:'a. P x /\ f x) = (?x:'a. h x))”,
      DISCH_TAC THEN DISCH_TAC THEN
      DISCH_THEN (fn rebuilt => REWRITE_TAC [GSYM rebuilt]) THEN
      AP_TERM_TAC THEN ABS_TAC THEN ASM_REWRITE_TAC [] THEN
      ASM_CASES_TAC “Q (x:'a) : bool” THEN RES_TAC THEN ASM_REWRITE_TAC []);
>>_ val ss = simpLib.++ (boolSimps.bool_ss,
     simpLib.threaded_cong_ss [threaded]);
>> simpLib.SIMP_CONV ss [ASSUME “!y:'a. bnd y ==> (bdy y <=> bdy' y)”]
     “?x:'a. bnd x /\ bdy x”;
```

